#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092082 :question/id #uuid "86835544-a7f1-5be2-99c1-14e39a14f3fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-603" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60627aba-89ef-53b6-b4ce-ca7854856bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236430} {:answer/id #uuid "70edbcb1-0834-5f95-9e7a-268c0a3c7bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236535} {:answer/id #uuid "806bb986-60b0-52f0-9e7d-fe440e12d7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236617} {:answer/id #uuid "d998f6fb-b8bd-532a-8961-d0fc2000cb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237195} {:answer/id #uuid "da1538df-e67c-5454-aadf-6bd6796416e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1538df-e67c-5454-aadf-6bd6796416e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237201} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb257275-f2f7-5467-9be9-cb2bc1b74739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237704}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcf46b6f-f5d9-5eec-afdf-9ca4614f945b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10255" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac040bc2c3eb943f947f34602c7c03e90f18e129a0fa7749d8ae60faaab12f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa3cd51-4469-5ba2-8636-0c4ac940fa23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235867} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9306d9-9701-5f33-a7ee-c92f9826deea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235990} {:answer/id #uuid "30704c9c-37d2-55f6-ba6e-a3279d9dc991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236126} {:answer/id #uuid "c65faa6c-8d42-5137-bf44-8cf565ae4758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237075} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb869b9-ae25-5d19-9d15-663789818115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9306d9-9701-5f33-a7ee-c92f9826deea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235990} :answer-field/id #uuid "25174e9d-1c45-51db-a656-8b3a0ad5d3c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "977bb89c-c84c-5262-ad82-39cc342b286c" :question/math-academy-id "q-10260" :question/problem "![](images/51/5117030d23370b2167513b7a2eee5258aae4eb9f45ad428fd82e4334c4ce6638.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1+3t,\\,y=2{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e58ea-86ce-5450-939e-02de03cdebfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} {:answer/id #uuid "2e56d505-7726-5af1-871c-95766070a7e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236112} {:answer/id #uuid "5b86c06b-4025-5e46-b183-b3a382a369a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236390} {:answer/id #uuid "62be5c5d-404d-5b63-b96a-01802a0419b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236442} {:answer/id #uuid "f134bbd3-d5b3-51e4-9905-9b65f60e5fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241e58ea-86ce-5450-939e-02de03cdebfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} :answer-field/id #uuid "86665b82-4f5e-59d4-b139-f213cd90fc89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237619}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeda4143-b348-582e-8960-ff5a4ad7dde2" :question/math-academy-id "q-10256" :question/problem "![](images/da/da529910e268ea4c124d7c27b085ca1799e255604459c539cc8d5809dda8f95a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a98e176-de94-501d-9654-ee79fa546361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235866} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbcf497-35c1-530e-8b8a-1e4520aff5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236401} {:answer/id #uuid "64a92041-6332-5b56-9a49-329279ae7898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236454} {:answer/id #uuid "c676c67b-ce2e-5ee3-a924-56c7b533c894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237076} {:answer/id #uuid "ea75476f-d42e-58e7-be1d-885fe5dc9408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea75476f-d42e-58e7-be1d-885fe5dc9408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8f08e3-d8d9-5787-8e9c-79e2c433c62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01f0c95b-ef07-5cce-9a1b-7f84a8200390" :question/math-academy-id "q-10257" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=2{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{cos}^{3}⁡t)=-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{3}⁡t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d582ab-25c3-5d89-b31c-eee9f2c1897c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236147} {:answer/id #uuid "671a11ec-27d8-52b9-9a59-8dd0d0ec54c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} {:answer/id #uuid "7dedda59-24a2-5ab6-bb9d-20a20b1f41ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236599} {:answer/id #uuid "c654b5a5-a703-55fd-9759-75e1cbdb9a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237074} {:answer/id #uuid "e227f87d-b8bb-5a30-92ed-b9b3c38e19ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671a11ec-27d8-52b9-9a59-8dd0d0ec54c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} :answer-field/id #uuid "58761fb6-2552-5c63-b655-9819d63debb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237557}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c17b75-37ea-5768-beae-14242dea6c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-10258" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,y=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa28b6-42d5-5647-8c67-6ad4ae934fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb02d36-4654-5167-a760-32462c48caf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235991} {:answer/id #uuid "3ead8f61-e137-5573-914b-52260b377c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236214} {:answer/id #uuid "478c7a12-33ef-5729-9381-971f513d4e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236277} {:answer/id #uuid "c3466be1-0698-5115-8533-dbaa1ef80524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09aa28b6-42d5-5647-8c67-6ad4ae934fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a5fcb-f5d2-5c81-be86-d5724d0bb948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237664}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31e80fe7-2790-57b6-8a22-f24ca436c4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-10261" :question/problem "![](images/82/8288453c2625dac592a51054df332abf8ee49dbf8cbcabaf327c3ffe63b55f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+4-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439511f-6bcb-5df1-b107-723ed7eac55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} {:answer/id #uuid "11540c92-a30f-5e29-852e-f44110b49f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284939} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd6e6c8-c066-57ed-aca5-d04d9fd31bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285371} {:answer/id #uuid "766a4044-6e95-5e6f-af50-99ef14df0af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285890} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d668e-b0ca-57c8-867a-44e3e4914dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0439511f-6bcb-5df1-b107-723ed7eac55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7cd0e3-04ea-5f3a-b65d-50397f5fe917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfd9b617-c165-5285-b91a-34485ed418b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20907" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413af386-018a-5a79-9059-adefc2791bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776} :answer-field/id #uuid "487a3ee4-df99-519f-a750-20dbe1bce54e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8e9f1bb-dfe9-519e-8bb3-e8a484b04ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348086" :question/problem "![](images/12/121012fc5671ada263e83ec6d3a897d187ecb3a9d56c8e4331a3c0c608a92622.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b79b-6298-5b7e-aa80-09f258e9f2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317907} :answer-field/id #uuid "1499962a-f058-50a8-a54d-83b434d7fef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c09908c-2ee7-5200-b887-daef1f475ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348088" :question/problem "![](images/be/be9e314ed11882842bbf9a74deeb3a994596c8275a4e854ffc66327fa0008058.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de455dde-6d11-5b5e-8482-288ea3511e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de455dde-6d11-5b5e-8482-288ea3511e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320067} :answer-field/id #uuid "4737cb78-73d4-5104-9fb5-39afec1f7dd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f48ecf1-07a6-51f5-a6dc-b4e6133b86d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351427" :question/problem "![](images/33/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3b7c9a-1628-5579-88b4-676c4b08825d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3b7c9a-1628-5579-88b4-676c4b08825d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319423} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1d24ee-bce7-5e94-b62a-b8bc141ef9ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320266}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4184edde-38d0-51b8-8041-b0992688a366" :question/math-academy-id "q-351428" :question/problem "![](images/da/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t+25{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df2cb4b-8bc6-564a-8b2d-5f0c079efa30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df2cb4b-8bc6-564a-8b2d-5f0c079efa30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319481} :answer-field/id #uuid "6098a653-b151-59fc-800d-89802b1f9a5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97b78e76-2eb3-551c-ab96-c9852dc794ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-351429" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fdcb137c459d5631db3dc3d02ff33ca0b791619694f1badbe7f3f2d0b19141d.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(2t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2t+1)^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t)^{2}+{((2t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}|t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462c106d-60a7-517a-bb34-02784bafa0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462c106d-60a7-517a-bb34-02784bafa0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319599} :answer-field/id #uuid "baadc580-16c9-5c86-999f-1612b836e8a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320545}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "212fc3da-e462-5e01-bc09-3c9becca9722" :question/math-academy-id "q-351438" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}+{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}({(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bf159b-7ee8-54ca-963e-4a6cee5dc607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bf159b-7ee8-54ca-963e-4a6cee5dc607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319938} :answer-field/id #uuid "580ca613-86fd-57f9-90d0-d5d1af6d384d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bc346f7-e655-5b4d-b5a3-0f310aa8fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351439" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{cos}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{sin}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+144{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1650979-8a8d-5c45-9228-031f33216db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1650979-8a8d-5c45-9228-031f33216db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320035} :answer-field/id #uuid "078922c3-552d-5dbd-b708-92b4116bb58c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "781cb0dd-c613-5bc2-b9c0-2614a05ddf91" :question/math-academy-id "q-351471" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4d2cf1820b792d804ab4d1b17ce1f36c0b63e4f406f8692d47f64a823b805f1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+9-18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-6(-1-1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335fc60d-9d0b-5146-9aa1-0fb071f12d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "335fc60d-9d0b-5146-9aa1-0fb071f12d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319550} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cab329-a431-597a-8084-a7aa60854288" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320203}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cc835fd-2da5-5e56-9fd0-7e642115b2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351474" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf06561072376949c6df49aec94b45c57b1cd43fbb031ea7b157ea5dfbac877a.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(6t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(6t+1)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd59335f-9ad0-5fc5-9249-f762bb6a6bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd59335f-9ad0-5fc5-9249-f762bb6a6bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320157} :answer-field/id #uuid "47988f9f-964f-5b0d-b582-4fcffe8e127c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320313}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d22cb6e-269c-50b3-a963-22e85d7e4b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-351505" :question/problem "![](images/89/893efa56ec41ef2242a48831ad4f58b23edc791464ccc7dcb4dd104b87843360.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1-{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1-2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(3+\\frac{27}{3})-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f98797f-08e7-52a2-b61b-fdbd3d8f6443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f98797f-08e7-52a2-b61b-fdbd3d8f6443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319486} :answer-field/id #uuid "1952a2da-3ef1-58fe-88d9-4df0661f32da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad2ad837-fe00-5eb7-ab02-db6f230cbbfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-351508" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a61d1a169971b5e0af320372885cdcbae794d163ab767685ef5b688a083aaadb.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2+4t,\\,y=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+4t)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2})=4{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(4{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16+16t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096410 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf8a11fa-1240-537a-95d9-4f13df04cb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 7 \\cdot e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094934} {:answer/id #uuid "dda24f68-a7ba-5bf1-96f0-617b7c17fe44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 7e^{x} + \\ln 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094935} {:answer/id #uuid "1309387e-621f-5d0b-bfb6-5f115748360a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094936} {:answer/id #uuid "2a07d543-9ac5-58f1-b5ef-aa2ee963c2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094937} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab10bca-83d1-523f-bea5-740b0fb72553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 7e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094938}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a83160c8-467b-5b39-bd2c-19bac5b891ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094933}] :question/id #uuid "c5e514ba-2b59-510b-8545-fa3adc383ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-1927" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 7 + 7e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ead02c-6b7a-5427-a098-9365a890c204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) =-4e^{x} + \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095821} {:answer/id #uuid "a2229a9e-ba02-54ac-8ee3-942cfe462c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095822} {:answer/id #uuid "da457f81-da86-59ef-a2f7-84fe5f19f924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095823} {:answer/id #uuid "11cb83da-303f-50b1-9867-145640b74f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2 - 4e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095824} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7daba6-5606-50b8-8ec8-465caa41dd60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) =-4e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095825}] :answer-field/id #uuid "42cc8fce-e292-5c51-b7cd-156991b55f2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095820}] :question/id #uuid "32d48a7b-7bcb-5f18-b8f4-b39f8f7670ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-51208" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 - 4e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f85df34-ee93-5aca-8d12-bcf5fe3faa9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236117} {:answer/id #uuid "4d519265-840b-5e03-a09d-732553549f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236310} {:answer/id #uuid "5283baad-0cc3-5be9-a599-fc7057b5e7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236339} {:answer/id #uuid "5848a3b7-55a7-59c9-957c-d8c9f1aede42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236373} {:answer/id #uuid "81168510-58be-58b5-8480-660231a90ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5848a3b7-55a7-59c9-957c-d8c9f1aede42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236373} :answer-field/id #uuid "e280c21e-3772-51fb-a46a-1fbd0e6c47ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237727}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "987874a9-79ce-51e5-b80a-f5a44c3f0800" :question/math-academy-id "q-101587" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=5{e}^{x}+1$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $1$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d31247c-4643-5464-a30c-802705aaf50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236007} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878615-e206-52a0-bf52-3f4f50ce31b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236190} {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} {:answer/id #uuid "9e126482-0792-53fb-9f81-3febb0cec7c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236807} {:answer/id #uuid "a16f91bd-4ea9-5f5b-b438-3d185dbe8f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea4542f-4e0e-5b2c-a84d-fa40f33689f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf78a5ba-4334-5b57-bc16-3a848e2f0dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-101590" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312717} {:answer/id #uuid "6cffecb4-1fcc-411a-8289-8bcd57cb4ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312717} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a411b7f-e09b-54e6-9330-1c2c71a4efc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314334}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6869b95a-63a5-54db-b062-b375ad3ac68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340056" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡8) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840} :answer-field/id #uuid "413c0c5a-e837-526a-8b03-fa256fc8b859" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19b7181e-4102-59d9-864a-c870a873bbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-340067" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡4$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡4) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡2} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2615d5d0-577d-51e9-a7eb-7299c88c1240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336388} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} {:answer/id #uuid "935cffee-f030-54ce-8980-0ae960bd62a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337376} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa127c-c35b-5236-91c3-effa79e34c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337635} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f526af-9e12-5d00-a48a-09643a747fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72ca8b2-1291-5b13-840e-71b284db3d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338635}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd4ec376-93b4-54c8-9a98-7640cf3d729e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71053" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=10-2{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $10$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \n                          f'(x)             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right)\\\n                                             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\n\t\t\t\t\t     &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\t\t\t\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  12x^{3} - 2x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de439e4-f2ba-5ae9-842a-f28efa8a537e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5e^{x} + 4x^{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093909} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbd038d-7002-5ba7-82c5-2d371f9a16e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5e^{x} + 4x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093910} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d1beda-b1bc-55e3-a236-e146a620eaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5e^{x} + 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093911} {:answer/id #uuid "5513b53e-594c-56c2-9cc0-15208c4e5665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5e^{x} + 4x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093912} {:answer/id #uuid "758222a3-6ee7-5064-863c-55b78f96db88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = e^{x} + 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093913}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7eaf35cc-b950-5185-a9a1-b26eb9ffff7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093908}] :question/id #uuid "be52e61b-18d3-573c-9b27-5eaa6fa86e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-11728" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 5e^{x} + 2x^{2} - 3$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96df9504-3ef2-55c1-b9e5-065414123c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = e^{x} - 18x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093915} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a0b264-2727-5885-9177-58162078b69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 9e^{x} - 18x^{2} + 11x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093916} {:answer/id #uuid "eeca2379-cc43-58fd-b608-2d6db5f34428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 9e^{x} - 18x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093917} {:answer/id #uuid "32190527-1131-5f56-9081-fff89da51730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 9e^{x} - 18x^{2} + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093918} {:answer/id #uuid "40a2b5e2-dd64-5f79-8261-69a8583333bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 9e^{x} - 18x^{2} + 11x - 20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093919}] :answer-field/id #uuid "053a4625-88a6-57b5-8eec-5cc8d2d0e629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093914}] :question/id #uuid "b6d07184-6bd9-5ef7-9517-a325fafb836a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11730" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 9e^{x} - 9x^{2} + 11x - 20$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7467eff-3284-5ff8-926d-e47188223d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7467eff-3284-5ff8-926d-e47188223d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234908} :answer-field/id #uuid "75fbad98-0a41-5480-ba06-edccdf81f980" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "413988df-903b-557d-87b6-8594e3b605b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167636" :question/problem "If $k(x)=g(x)-f(x),$ where $A=2$ and $B=3,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 3 and 4, respectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =g(x)-f(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-({x}^{3}+2x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the addition and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}k}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58606740-b081-5671-ab93-5b28309b7d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58606740-b081-5671-ab93-5b28309b7d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bd4a86-aed0-5919-98b7-ad23d53bddbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfc04756-ffae-5a03-8a05-d0ea5dfb5207" :question/math-academy-id "q-167694" :question/problem "For $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "For $x<1,$ we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x,$ which is differentiable.\n\nUsing the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0884ad38-9d14-5f8e-b665-d8b35b5af237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269346} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ac08-c4ce-5d3f-845f-d38358413506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269388} {:answer/id #uuid "2934301c-7684-5c1f-b910-d7819431eba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269640} {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} {:answer/id #uuid "81639cc6-e903-5cd2-bc14-50bfc72b4d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c2979c-8072-5209-9ca0-20ccbaf77003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2ffa4af-5dbe-569b-8d78-f233584a8913" :question/math-academy-id "q-140431" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x}+8x)\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{1}+8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2e+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1859eb59-5659-5101-b949-46d66d579831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269472} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0e81a7-1d07-50d3-b00e-dd0c50955d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269501} {:answer/id #uuid "2d71f9f8-72d8-5409-b4ca-0645cd6983b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269679} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ca486a-dc13-5bb9-932e-532847144371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270990} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a796d4-403d-5aad-82ff-004acd0b4a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a796d4-403d-5aad-82ff-004acd0b4a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271012} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c8b5dd-9fdc-5213-98ff-f269c094b6e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271882}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "578adda7-12d4-5f1e-a8c7-f591ce6d42cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140432" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+6{e}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2x+6{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x+2+6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(2x+2+6{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+2+6\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397833d7-9cc6-50ae-8de7-3a5674155a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282196} {:answer/id #uuid "8d66fe18-2e0a-548c-a20c-052aa8a29cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282914} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc41fd0-6fd9-5709-9af2-b2dd6b711472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283503} {:answer/id #uuid "df2ebee4-e1b4-5f85-bbbc-9c810b46a256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283637} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32be51-d0ac-5644-92b4-204731798028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2ebee4-e1b4-5f85-bbbc-9c810b46a256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283637} :answer-field/id #uuid "34cf92cc-bcc5-56af-93bf-714a48258bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284027}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5816a349-a320-5d09-93fc-2ee07a9147af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17247" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-3(2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(2{e}^{x}-6x)\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{0}-6\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ad567a-37aa-5c67-8461-48b832a97b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282410} {:answer/id #uuid "84f495d5-d4be-5db4-8f35-da2bca8cfe2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282836} {:answer/id #uuid "9e412a36-003f-51b6-80db-13d3f13969a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283068} {:answer/id #uuid "a99e9fb3-9911-531b-94e1-18309a3f6530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283157} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf7484-ada1-5d36-86d4-7dceaa4a2600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ad567a-37aa-5c67-8461-48b832a97b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282410} :answer-field/id #uuid "114801c2-0284-5663-a95f-f2fe249472cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283967}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7bb16ef-b727-58e4-9164-1a907f3660c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-17255" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=11{e}^{x}+8{x}^{2}-12x-20$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11{e}^{x}+8{x}^{2}-12x-20) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\  & =11\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\  & =11{e}^{x}+8(2x)-12(1)-0 \\\\  & =11{e}^{x}+16x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(11{e}^{x}+16x-12)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =11{e}^{0}+16\\cdot 0-12 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 1+0-12 \\\\  & =11-12 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b89210db-d5da-5a48-8355-741becb1873c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296232} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f887ee-f564-4711-a252-4f52754bf8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42x^6+2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89210db-d5da-5a48-8355-741becb1873c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296232} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca439fbc-124d-5dae-b8b3-a85cb6575e03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f66d07e-3ca1-595a-b1d7-b4c775329676" :question/math-academy-id "q-304035" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=6{x}^{7}+2{e}^{x}$, the exact value of the slope of the tangent line at $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{7}+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =6(7{x}^{6})+2{e}^{x} \\\\  & =42{x}^{6}+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(42{x}^{6}+2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =42(-1)^{6}+2{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =42\\cdot 1+\\frac{2}{e} \\\\  & =42+\\frac{2}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14e68965-6c39-5947-ac05-eee99e685063" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d843f8b3-130e-58a5-9fbd-82ec568db2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5 \\cdot 2^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093921} {:answer/id #uuid "014e0413-2e84-53f7-b45a-ddf3de5c66ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\frac{5 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093922} {:answer/id #uuid "59ffeb59-c600-576e-b386-4238d5be4435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\frac{5\\ln 2}{2^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093923} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7bb843-7ffc-5cee-ae0d-c06b53bc9748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 5\\ln 2 \\cdot 2^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093924}] :answer-field/id #uuid "391923fc-8733-5f78-b82d-8361511793c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093920}] :question/id #uuid "991df192-1bbb-5ddd-b9f8-9c449c0a2bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11732" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 5 \\cdot 2^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84fac42b-8598-507e-a597-b51cf44552e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 4^{x}\\ln 4 + 3e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094904} {:answer/id #uuid "9f573042-c991-5b93-95cd-f442a342ef79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 4 + 3e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094905} {:answer/id #uuid "a83c0a13-5121-5e13-b635-203e453d1fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 4^{x} - \\ln 4 + 3e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094906} {:answer/id #uuid "e39ac619-c915-5405-9fdf-5d74bfe05e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 4^{x}\\ln x + 3e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094907} {:answer/id #uuid "71daaeac-c06c-5a66-a1ba-95951dbcd11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 4^{x} + 3e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094908}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4b148928-ba92-5c24-b71d-675d583ec080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094903}] :question/id #uuid "e0af69f1-1a58-5ac7-90e2-c2859723b842" :question/math-academy-id "q-1917" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 4^{x} + 3e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3232bdcf-d781-5e59-bb5b-56c10f0d7fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}ln⁡6-10{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250976} {:answer/id #uuid "37865371-b061-54aa-9810-81e59d0b835b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}-10{x}^{2}+6x-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251009} {:answer/id #uuid "3d09661f-ce26-5e19-a8b8-2ae50263662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}ln⁡6-10x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251060} {:answer/id #uuid "9007950f-ce55-5f0c-a1f6-d3963f0294f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}-10x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251775} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf32e0c-8341-5060-b67e-c44870ab4cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}ln⁡6-10{x}^{2}+6x-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d09661f-ce26-5e19-a8b8-2ae50263662e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{x}ln⁡6-10x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251060} :answer-field/id #uuid "e849ca6e-9bda-5244-a669-0f22826facaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50678a5c-afcb-522f-98fb-80e9680afca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11735" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={6}^{x}-5{x}^{2}+6x-19$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x}-5{x}^{2}+6x-19) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(19) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(19) \\\\  & =ln⁡6\\cdot {6}^{x}-5(2x)+6(1)-0 \\\\  & ={6}^{x}ln⁡6-10x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbb468a-9928-5c35-89ed-eaf197f78886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281954} {:answer/id #uuid "618693f7-594d-59ce-a534-76a8e9e2b379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6635-e022-5c1a-a6c7-78955c8928cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282867} {:answer/id #uuid "8957ae1d-f55e-532c-822e-e38dcfa2b32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282874} {:answer/id #uuid "956605d2-ce2e-57d7-b286-ab43b24751bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "618693f7-594d-59ce-a534-76a8e9e2b379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e9ba42-2c84-571b-94b9-4df571509ab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284227}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e51c642-d912-5d35-8cf2-7c273fba130d" :question/math-academy-id "q-17388" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}+5{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}ln⁡3+5{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={({3}^{x}ln⁡3+5{e}^{x})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & ={3}^{0}ln⁡3+5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot ln⁡3+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =ln⁡3+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3554bc38-45ed-5174-b2e0-b7a82dde78d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}(8){8}^{x}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3554bc38-45ed-5174-b2e0-b7a82dde78d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}(8){8}^{x}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295565} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6fdabb-ec35-5bee-8e6f-6b6e93c3ccd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df22161-f9b7-5077-b623-7a70a19c609f" :question/math-academy-id "q-304038" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x})+12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3(ln⁡(8){8}^{x})+12(1)+0 \\\\  & =3ln⁡(8){8}^{x}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202a3876-df45-5f69-9dea-3b48cc477974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}(8){8}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312218} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad5377e-557d-4b02-81f5-eba612380c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot8^{x}\\ln8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202a3876-df45-5f69-9dea-3b48cc477974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}(8){8}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5168e7a2-5958-57af-b48a-ff61e1073f47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca876738-9b8d-5832-85d7-2d09df78130a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340272" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=7-2\\cdot {8}^{x}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-2\\cdot {8}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\cdot {8}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2(ln⁡(8){8}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(8){8}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40066746-e699-53ed-82fd-0f35f8e924c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(5)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312491} {:answer/id #uuid "8688c450-e972-4ef8-abf7-296b751229f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5^{x}\\ln5-18x^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40066746-e699-53ed-82fd-0f35f8e924c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(5)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312491} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ba52fe-3ccd-59f7-bca4-208b03b18375" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3624c-f62d-5f81-b0de-c13d8e4bf17c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340280" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}-6{x}^{3}+4x-1$, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}-6{x}^{3}+4x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-6(3{x}^{2})+4(1)-0 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-18{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-18{x}^{2}+4)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{0}-18(0)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot 1-0+4 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}   & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)'\\  \n                                                    & = 5 (\\ln x)'\\\n                                                    &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n                                                    &= \\dfrac{5}{x}.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t  \n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957a337d-5366-5ff2-837c-52484ac86768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094100} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a5634d-31a1-562b-a09a-feb05e88887c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094101} {:answer/id #uuid "4832dd38-53a9-5b87-8cd8-b896e7db9350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094102} {:answer/id #uuid "542026ef-81ef-58c0-a96c-df6b38bc70ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094103} {:answer/id #uuid "67a422d5-e423-5c10-8c25-5930bf5bc45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094104}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c7111e8a-6b0b-59ab-9d87-0933257a4569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094099}] :question/id #uuid "39f62811-3b9a-5225-ae53-715b926ed4a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-11909" :question/problem "Given that $y = 7\\ln x$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd19f85a-2650-57ea-8cf3-72f238d98cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094106} {:answer/id #uuid "4697884b-8a68-5b25-ae48-ac52961a3956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094107} {:answer/id #uuid "54eea33d-179b-5ebb-8bca-781adbf1b670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094108} {:answer/id #uuid "71f262c4-9cec-5233-a791-8fa255f01f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094109} {:answer/id #uuid "7e239cc3-973d-55a9-bc44-d2fef16d3346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094110}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a8b73a-f5ca-5ea4-8736-6d1bbec6c71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094105}] :question/id #uuid "5519d8f3-5f4f-5b34-b1bb-b26079a6662a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11910" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\frac{2}{3}\\ln x$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6bba1a6-7ea1-5b05-9ffb-69c67a87c5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bba1a6-7ea1-5b05-9ffb-69c67a87c5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232802} :answer-field/id #uuid "e320e47d-cc25-5e34-a058-d86a0416a0d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232801}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6a48b7a-f270-5ba0-b480-17bea4dd2da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339941" :question/problem "Given that $y=-11\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234288 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "531eff6b-7728-5135-92da-8477ae71ccbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70881" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1d6f3e-9053-5690-b000-66d554fc0b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236110} {:answer/id #uuid "3532faea-48d8-5741-aa13-a64cbba7d647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ex}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236153} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1aa2e2-8a76-5713-9b50-46a772b85443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236316} {:answer/id #uuid "a1648056-d8f0-59f6-985d-fcda1cb5175d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{e}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236828} {:answer/id #uuid "d8be5480-6829-5df9-a0e9-113723bfaa4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e1aa2e2-8a76-5713-9b50-46a772b85443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236316} :answer-field/id #uuid "994834f1-4e9a-532e-b583-a55ce29002bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237641}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "319b8cc3-c619-5308-8487-fad516398fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-101600" :question/problem "Given that $y=2eln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2eln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5235b2d2-41a6-571c-8e01-a03aeb01451b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236336} {:answer/id #uuid "7f36f138-72b0-5be3-99c0-4538ad3f84d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236605} {:answer/id #uuid "a14212af-f2a6-570c-8589-28ef6e251469" 